    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT CASE STUDIES DO YOU SUPPORT</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Four are bundled as worked examples:

• Oyster — an oyster-card style transit ticket protocol.
• SSO — a single sign-on flow between an identity provider and a relying party.
• Coach Service — a multi-party booking protocol with cooperating services.
• Bank Login — a banking-app authentication ceremony with a second factor.

Each one has been used to validate the mutation engine end to end, so they are good starting points if you want to see what the output looks like before pointing the tool at your own theory.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] What is Oyster? | What is SSO? | What is Coach Service? | What is Bank Login?]]></template>
    </category>

    <category><pattern>WHAT CASE STUDIES</pattern><template><srai>WHAT CASE STUDIES DO YOU SUPPORT</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>WHAT EXAMPLES ARE THERE</pattern><template><srai>WHAT CASE STUDIES DO YOU SUPPORT</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>LIST CASE STUDIES</pattern><template><srai>WHAT CASE STUDIES DO YOU SUPPORT</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT IS OYSTER</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Oyster is a transit-ticket ceremony modelled after contactless-card systems. The interesting properties are unlinkability of card uses and integrity of the fare ledger. It is a good first target for Skip and Forget — small enough to iterate fast, structured enough to surface real attacks when a check is dropped.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] What case studies do you support? | What is SSO? | What is Bank Login?]]></template>
    </category>
    <category><pattern>OYSTER</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS OYSTER</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT IS SSO</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[SSO is the single sign-on case study: a user authenticates once with an identity provider, then logs into a relying-party service using the resulting token. Security goals centre on token integrity and the relying party's authentication of the user. Replace Type often turns up token-confusion attacks here; Forget is good for testing what happens if a long-term key rolls over mid-session.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] What case studies do you support? | What is Replace Type? | What is Forget?]]></template>
    </category>
    <category><pattern>SSO</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS SSO</srai></template></category>
    <category><pattern>SINGLE SIGN ON</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS SSO</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT IS COACH SERVICE</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Coach Service is a multi-party booking ceremony — a passenger, a booking front-end, and a coach operator all need to agree on a ticket and a seat. It exercises mutations that touch cross-service messages: Add and Combine often surface here, because the realistic failure mode is "one service tells another a bit too much".

[[FOLLOWUPS]] What case studies do you support? | What is Add? | What is Combine?]]></template>
    </category>
    <category><pattern>COACH SERVICE</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS COACH SERVICE</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>WHAT IS BANK LOGIN</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[Bank Login models a banking-app login ceremony with a second factor. The questions worth asking are: does the second factor actually bind to the session (test with Replace Type), and does the protocol still authenticate if the second-factor check is silently dropped (test with Neglect). Forget is also informative here — it models a phone losing the second-factor token mid-handshake.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] What case studies do you support? | What is Replace Type? | What is Neglect?]]></template>
    </category>
    <category><pattern>BANK LOGIN</pattern><template><srai>WHAT IS BANK LOGIN</srai></template></category>

    <category>
        <pattern>HOW LONG WILL THE ANALYSIS TAKE</pattern>
        <template><![CDATA[It depends almost entirely on which mutations you tick. Rough order of magnitude on a modest case study:

• Skip — seconds per mutant.
• Add, Replace, Combine — seconds to a minute per mutant.
• Neglect — comparable to Replace.
• Forget with Limited Depth — under a minute.
• Forget with Infinite Depth — minutes to tens of minutes, occasionally longer if the derivation space is rich.

If a run is dragging, prefer Limited Depth or set an explicit Depth before re-running.

[[FOLLOWUPS]] Which mutation is fastest? | Which mutation is slowest? | What is Forget?]]></template>
    </category>

